### How does TreeSet with a comparator work?

`TreeSet` uses a comparator (or natural ordering) to both sort and determine uniqueness. When you supply a salary comparator, the set sorts employees by salary and treats two employees as equal if their salaries compare as equal. This means employees with the same salary are silently deduplicated.

### How do you implement it?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class TreeSetCollector {

    record Employee(String id, String name, BigDecimal salary) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
            new Employee("SAV2001", "Rajesh",  new BigDecimal("1800000")),
            new Employee("SAV2002", "Priya",   new BigDecimal("1000000")),
            new Employee("SAV2003", "Kavita",  new BigDecimal("1000000")),
            new Employee("SAV2004", "Deepak",  new BigDecimal("1500000"))
        );

        // DANGEROUS: salary-only comparator drops Kavita (same salary as Priya)
        TreeSet<Employee> buggy = staff.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toCollection(
                () -> new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary))));
        System.out.println("Buggy count: " + buggy.size());  // 3, not 4!

        // CORRECT: add tie-breaker to keep distinct employees
        TreeSet<Employee> correct = staff.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toCollection(
                () -> new TreeSet<>(
                    Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)
                        .thenComparing(Employee::id))));
        System.out.println("Correct count: " + correct.size());  // 4

        correct.forEach(e ->
            System.out.println("  " + e.name() + ": Rs." + e.salary()));
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy count: 3
Correct count: 4
  Priya: Rs.1000000
  Kavita: Rs.1000000
  Deepak: Rs.1500000
  Rajesh: Rs.1800000
```

### Why does the salary-only comparator drop employees?

TreeSet uses the comparator for equality: if `comparator.compare(a, b) == 0`, the elements are considered the same and one is discarded. With a salary-only comparator, Priya and Kavita both earn Rs.1,000,000, so `compare(Priya, Kavita) == 0` and one is dropped.

### How is TreeSet equality different from equals()?

| Aspect | `TreeSet` (comparator) | `HashSet` (equals/hashCode) |
|---|---|---|
| Equality check | `comparator.compare(a, b) == 0` | `a.equals(b) && a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()` |
| Sorting | Yes (by comparator) | No (unordered) |
| Same-salary employees | Treated as duplicates (if no tie-breaker) | Distinct (different IDs) |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Using a salary-only comparator and silently losing data.

```java
// WRONG: same salary = same employee in TreeSet
new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary))
// Priya and Kavita (both Rs.1M) → only one kept

// CORRECT: add a tie-breaker
new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)
    .thenComparing(Employee::id))
// Both Priya and Kavita kept, sorted by salary then by ID
```

### When should you use toCollection vs toSet?

`toSet()` gives no control over the concrete type or ordering. `toCollection(supplier)` lets you choose exactly the collection type and comparator.

```java
// toSet(): HashSet, no order, no comparator
Set<Employee> hashSet = staff.stream().collect(Collectors.toSet());

// toCollection(): TreeSet, salary-sorted, with tie-breaker
TreeSet<Employee> treeSet = staff.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toCollection(
        () -> new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary)
            .thenComparing(Employee::id))));
```

### What about using sorted().toList() as an alternative?

If you do not need deduplication and just want a sorted list, `sorted(comparator).toList()` is simpler and does not have the comparator-equality trap.

```java
List<Employee> sortedList = staff.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::salary))
    .toList();
// All employees kept, sorted by salary
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The comparator-equality pitfall is a classic TreeSet trap. A senior engineer always adds a tie-breaker to the comparator when collecting into a TreeSet, ensuring that genuinely different elements are not silently dropped. When deduplication is not desired, using `sorted().toList()` avoids the trap entirely.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What happens if two employees have the same salary?" With salary-only comparator, one is dropped. Add a tie-breaker.
- "How is TreeSet equality different from equals?" TreeSet uses the comparator, not `equals`.
- "When should you use TreeSet vs sorted().toList()?" TreeSet when you want sorted deduplication; sorted list when you want all elements sorted.
